Myth: The Merchant and the Ferryman

Long ago, a merchant came to a wide river carrying three coins of silver. On the far shore lay the market where her goods would triple in value.

The ferryman said: "Give me what coins you will. I shall carry them across, and the river spirits will multiply them threefold. Then I choose how many to return to you."

The merchant weighed her coins. To give none meant no crossing, no gain. To give all meant complete vulnerability—what if the ferryman kept everything?

She placed three coins in his weathered palm. "This is half my fortune," she said. "I trust you will honor the balance between us."

The ferryman felt the weight of those coins—not just silver, but vulnerability, hope, and measured faith. Nine coins materialized in his boat, shimmering with river magic.

He stood at the far shore, holding her multiplied fortune. Would he return fairly and build lasting partnership? Would he return everything, proving himself trustworthy but leaving nothing for himself? Or would he keep it all and destroy possibility forever?

The merchant waited on her shore, having made her choice.

Now the ferryman had to make his.

The river watched, and recorded, and waited.
