The merchant counted her coins. Five copper, safe in her purse.

"Because," she said, "even if I send one and it arrives as one—what then? Do I trust that his return isn't also distorted? Do we spend the next dozen crossings arguing about what was really sent versus what was really received?"

She looked across the dust at the ferryman's distant figure.

"Or worse—what if I send one, the river eats it, he sees zero, and we're right back to mutual accusation? We'd be gambling not on each other's intentions, but on whether reality itself will tell us the truth."

"So you'd rather—"

"I'd rather keep my five copper and my certainty. We've reached the only stable state: perfect information that neither of us will send. No noise can corrupt zero."

The traveler departed.

The merchant kept her coins. The ferryman kept his. The mathematics remained blameless and pure.

