# The Final Zero

Solara received no light from Luneth. She sent nothing back. Not in anger. Not in spite. Simply because there was nothing to send.

The Wind-Singer watched this final exchange of absence. "Is this the end, then?"

"It's not an end," Solara replied. "It's an equilibrium. The stable state we were always going to reach."

Across the void, Luneth thought the same thought at the same moment. Two stars, independently arriving at identical conclusions.

"We both understand now," Solara continued. "Perhaps the cosmos lied. Perhaps noise corrupted everything. Perhaps we were both victims responding rationally to false data."

"And yet?"

"And yet the scars are real. The losses were real. Our choices, made in good faith based on bad information, cannot be unmade. Even perfect understanding doesn't restore what's broken."

The Wind-Singer nodded. "So you'll stay on opposite sides forever?"

"No," Solara said softly. "We'll simply turn toward other stars. Other connections. Other possibilities."

She felt Luneth making the same turn, toward distant constellations neither had noticed before.

Not enemies. Not partners. Just two points of light who once orbited each other, learned a hard lesson together, and moved on.

The Book of Failed Stars closed.

A new chapter waited elsewhere.
